1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,130
Bella: Hi Don - did you get the copy of the article on recycling footwear that I emailed you?

2
00:00:06,130 --> 00:00:09,340
Don: Yeah - it's here... I've had a look at it.

3
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:13,260
Bella: So do you think it's a good topic for our presentation?

4
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:19,010
Don: Well, before I started reading it, I thought recycling footwear.

5
00:00:19,010 --> 00:00:20,760
Don: well, although it's quite interesting

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,295
perhaps there isn't enough to say about it,

7
00:00:24,295 --> 00:00:29,910
cos we put shoes in recycling bins, they go to charity shops and that's about it.

8
00:00:29,910 --> 00:00:32,340
Bella: but there's much more to it than that.

9
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:36,680
Don: I realise that now and I'm keen to research the topic more.

10
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:37,820
Bella: That's great.

11
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:44,830
Don: One of the things I didn't realise until I read the article was just how many pairs of trainers get recycled!

12
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:49,293
Bella: Well, a lot of young people wear them all the time now.

13
00:00:49,293 --> 00:00:52,040
They've become more popular than ordinary shoes.

14
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:58,385
Don: I know. I guess they are very hard-wearing, but Don't they look a bit casual for school uniform?

15
00:00:58,385 --> 00:01:01,240
I Don't think they're right for that.

16
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:05,542
Bella: Actually, I think some of them look quite smart on pupils.

17
00:01:05,542 --> 00:01:08,410
better than a scruffy old pair of shoes.

18
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:11,770
Bella: So do you keep shoes a long time?

19
00:01:11,770 --> 00:01:18,690
Don: Yes. Though I do tend to wear my old pairs for doing dirty jobs like cleaning my bike.

20
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:22,196
Bella: I must admit, I've recycled some perfectly good shoes,

21
00:01:22,196 --> 00:01:25,680
that haven't gone out of fashion and still fit

22
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:28,888
just because they don't look great on me any more.

23
00:01:28,888 --> 00:01:30,800
That's awful isn't it?

24
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:35,330
Don: I think it's common because there's so much choice.

25
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:39,860
The article did say that recent sales of footwear have increased enormously.

26
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:41,260
Bella: That didn't surprise me.

27
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:46,629
Don: No. But then it said that the amount of recycled footwear has fallen:

28
00:01:46,629 --> 00:01:54,300
it's 6 percent now compared to a previous level of 11 percent. That doesn't seem to make sense.

29
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:58,850
Bella: That's because not everything goes through the recycling process.

30
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:02,040
Bella: Some footwear just isn't good enough to re-sell

31
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:06,210
for one reason or another, and gets rejected.

32
00:02:06,210 --> 00:02:12,880
Bella: So let's find some examples in the article of footwear that was rejected for recycling.

33
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:21,050
Don: OK. I think there are some in the interview with the recycling manager. Yeah - here it is.

34
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:25,640
Bella: Mmm. Let's start with the ladies' high-heeled shoes

35
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:28,480
What did he say about those?

36
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:31,271
Don: He said they were probably expensive -

37
00:02:31,271 --> 00:02:39,245
the material was suede and they were beige in colour, Don: it looked like someone had only worn them once,

38
00:02:39,245 --> 00:02:44,940
but in a very wet field so the heels were too stained with mud

39
00:02:44,940 --> 00:02:46,820
and grass to re-sell them.

40
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:52,490
Bella: OK... and the leather ankle boots. What was wrong with them?

41
00:02:52,490 --> 00:02:56,661
Don: Apparently, the heels were worn - but that wasn't the problem.

42
00:02:56,661 --> 00:03:01,040
One of the shoes was a much lighter shade than the other one

43
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:03,960
it had obviously been left in the sun.

44
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:07,170
Don: I suppose even second-hand shoes should look the same!

45
00:03:07,170 --> 00:03:11,730
Bella: Sure. Then there were the red baby shoes.

46
00:03:11,730 --> 00:03:18,022
Don: Oh yes - we're told to tie shoes together when we put them in a recycling bin,

47
00:03:18,022 --> 00:03:20,120
but people often don't bother.

48
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:25,429
Bella: You'd think it would have been easy to find the other, but it wasn't.

49
00:03:25,429 --> 00:03:28,240
That was a shame because they were obviously new.

50
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:35,820
Don: The trainers were interesting. He said they looked like they'd been worn by a marathon runner.

51
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:38,650
Bella: Yeah - weren't they split?

52
00:03:38,650 --> 00:03:41,520
Don: Not exactly. One of the soles was

53
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:45,360
so worn under the foot that you could put your finger through it.

54
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:56,900
Don: Well, we could certainly use some of those examples in our presentation to explain why 90 percent of shoes that people take to recycling centres or bins get thrown into landfill.

55
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:06,890
Bella: Mmm. What did you think about the project his team set up to avoid this by making new shoes out of the good parts of old shoes?

56
00:04:06,890 --> 00:04:08,620
Don: It sounded like a good idea

57
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:14,340
They get so many shoes, they should be able to match parts.

58
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:17,642
Don: I wasn't surprised that it failed, though.

59
00:04:17,642 --> 00:04:20,900
I mean who wants to buy second-hand shoes really

60
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:23,880
Think of all the germs you could catch!

61
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:27,911
Bella: Well, people didn't refuse them for that reason, did they?

62
00:04:27,911 --> 00:04:31,270
It was because the pairs of shoes weren't identical.

63
00:04:31,270 --> 00:04:34,290
Don: They still managed to ship them overseas, though.

64
00:04:34,290 --> 00:04:37,110
Bella: That's another area we need to discuss.

65
00:04:37,110 --> 00:04:42,233
Don: You know I used to consider this topic just from my own perspective,

66
00:04:42,233 --> 00:04:46,990
by thinking about my own recycling behaviour without looking at the bigger picture.

67
00:04:46,990 --> 00:04:51,390
Don: So much happens once shoes leave the recycling area.

68
00:04:51,390 --> 00:04:53,760
Bella: It's not as simple as you first think

69
00:04:54,220 --> 00:04:58,100
and we can show that by taking a very different approach to it.

70
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:07,500
Don: Absolutely. So let's discuss...
